{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And he called his name Er</b> — Judah called his son Er, from the root of \"You have stirred up Your might\" (Psalms 80:3). And his wife called the name of the second one Onan, but Scripture did not report why. Now it is possible that she linked it to her giving birth, for it is the way of women to name their children on this basis, like the mother of Jabez (1 Chronicles 4:9), who called his name Jabez, \"saying, Because I bore him in pain,\" and likewise Atarah the mother of Onam did (ibid. 2:26). And it is from the root of \"And the people were as murmurers\" (Numbers 11:1), \"Why should a living man complain\" (Lamentations 3:39), like Ben-Oni of Rachel (Genesis 35:18). And Judah did not insist on changing his name as Jacob his father did. And in Bereshit Rabbah (Bereshit Rabbah 85:4) they said: Er — because he was uprooted (הוּעַר) from the world. And this was not Judah's intention, but they expounded that the names allude to what would come."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 38, :notes {0 "Ramban cites Bereshit Rabbah as פ״ה:ד׳ (chapter 85:4); resolved by standard parashah–verse numbering for this parashah."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:41:29.901999659Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}